==Latin==
{{wikipedia|lang=la}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|la|grc|Χ|t=chi}}, which represented /ks/ in the dialects of most of the Greek mainland and Euboea.

===Pronunciation===
The sound of X was like that of the Greek {{m|el|Ξ|t=xi}}, that is /ks/, although etymologically it represented not only ''cs'' (as in {{l|la|lūx}}, from luc-s, and {{l|la|dīxī}}, from dic-si), but also ''gs'' (as in {{l|la|lēx}}, from leg-s; {{l|la|rēxī}}, from reg-si); ''hs'' (as in {{l|la|trāxī}}, from trah-si; {{l|la|vexī}}, from veh-si); and ''chs'' (as in the word {{l|la|onyx}}, from onych-s, borrowed from the Greek). The
hardening of a softer final (''g'', ''h'', ''ch'') before ''s'' into the ''с'' sound, which occurs in the last-mentioned cases, is found also in several roots ending in ''v'' and ''u'': {{l|la|nix}} for ''niv-s'', {{l|la|vīxī}} for ''viv-si'', {{l|la|connixī}} for conniv-si, {{l|la|fluxī}} for ''fluv-si'', from {{l|la|fluō}} (root ''fluv-''; compare {{l|la|fluvius}}), {{l|la|struxī}} for ''stru-si''.

Less frequently ''x'' has arisen from the combinations ''ps'' and ''ts'': {{l|la|proximus}} for ''prop-simus'' (from {{l|la|prope}}), {{l|la|nīxus}} for nit-sus (from {{l|la|nītor}}), the latter being used along with the collateral form {{l|la|nīsus}}, as also {{l|la|connīvī}} with {{l|la|connixī}}, and {{l|la|mistus}} (from {{l|la|misceō}}) with {{l|la|mixtus}}.

An exchange of the sounds ''ss'' or ''s'' and ''x'', took place in {{l|la|axis}} for ''assis'' and {{l|la|laxus}} for ''lassus''. In the later language of the vulgar, the guttural sound in ''x'' disappeared, and ''s'' or ''ss'' was often written for it; as ''vis'' for {{l|la|vix}}, ''visit'' for {{l|la|vīxit}}, ''unsit'' for {{l|la|unxit}}, conflississet for {{l|la|conflixisset}}, in late inscriptions; hence regularly in Italian, and frequently in the other Romance tongues, the Latin ''x'' is represented by ''s'' or ''ss''.

By a mere graphic variation, one of the constituent sounds of ''x'' is often expressed in inscriptions (but not the earliest) by an additional ''с'' or ''s''; as SACXO or SAXSO for {{l|la|saxō}}; VCXOR or VXSOR for {{l|la|uxor}}; CONIVNCX or CONIVNXS for {{l|la|conjunx}}; even both sounds are sometimes thus expressed, VICXSIT for {{l|la|vīxit}}.

